<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/victorian-house.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/victorian-house-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/victorian-house.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.15"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><a href="/where-we-work/">Where we work</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Scranton</span></nav><p class="kicker">Northeastern PA</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Scranton, PA</h1><p class="lede">Scranton was built on anthracite, and the old mines are still under much of the valley. Add heavy winters and a hilly, older housing stock, and Lackawanna County claims need careful handling.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line-light" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Lackawanna County</dd></div><div><dt>Housing</dt><dd>Victorians, double blocks &amp; frame homes</dd></div><div><dt>Storm on record</dt><dd>Tropical Storm Lee, Sept 2011</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<div class="peril-box"><p class="peril-h">Common local claim risks</p><ul class="chips"><li>Mine subsidence</li><li>Heavy snow &amp; ice dams</li><li>Frozen pipes</li><li>Flash flooding</li><li>Wind &amp; fallen trees</li></ul></div>

<h2>Living above the mines</h2>
<p>Coal mining shaped the Lackawanna Valley for generations, and the tunnels and voids it left behind sit beneath large parts of Scranton, Dunmore, Taylor, Old Forge, Throop and Dickson City. When old workings collapse, the ground above can settle, cracking foundations, racking door frames and splitting walls.</p>
<p>Standard homeowner policies usually exclude earth movement, including mine subsidence. The Pennsylvania Department of Environmental Protection runs the Mine Subsidence Insurance (MSI) program, which offers separate coverage for damage caused by underground coal mine collapse. It's inexpensive compared with most coverage, and many valley homeowners carry it. If you have damage that looks like settling, it's worth knowing which policy you have and how each one defines the loss. Our <a href="/policy-and-claim-guides/mine-subsidence-sinkholes-and-your-policy/">mine subsidence and sinkholes guide</a> lays out the basics. Coverage disputes are a question for a Pennsylvania attorney; appraisal is for disagreements about the amount of a covered loss.</p>
<h2>Housing on Scranton's hills</h2>
<p>The Hill Section and Green Ridge are known for large Victorian and early-twentieth-century homes, many with slate roofs, wraparound porches and detailed woodwork. West Scranton, South Side, Minooka, Providence and Bull's Head have streets of frame homes and double blocks, the side-by-side two-family houses common across northeastern Pennsylvania. North toward Clarks Summit and Abington, housing turns more suburban.</p>
<p>Double blocks and older frame homes present a few recurring problems in claims:</p>
<ul>
<li>One roof over two units, with two owners or a landlord and tenants involved.</li>
<li>Slate and specialty materials that the carrier wants to replace with something cheaper.</li>
<li>Siding that can't be matched, raising the question of how much of a wall or elevation is replaced.</li>
<li>Porches and balconies with structural damage hidden behind trim.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Winters that test roofs and plumbing</h2>
<p>Northeastern Pennsylvania winters are long. Snow piles up on north-facing roofs, ice dams form along eaves, and pipes in exterior walls and unheated porches freeze. The Blizzard of 1993 and the Valentine's Day 2007 storm are still remembered here. We regularly see ice dam leaks paid for drywall but not for wet insulation, flooring or the roof edge work needed to stop the next leak. Our <a href="/property-appraisal-services/ice-dam-frozen-pipe-appraisal/">ice dam and frozen pipe appraisal</a> service is built for these claims.</p>
<h2>Floods along the Lackawanna</h2>
<p>The Lackawanna River and creeks like Roaring Brook and Stafford Meadow Brook can rise quickly. Tropical Storm Lee in September 2011 brought serious flooding to parts of the valley. Flood from rising water is excluded from homeowner policies and goes through a flood policy such as NFIP, which has its own process; homeowner appraisal doesn't cover it. Covered parts of the same storm, such as wind damage, roof leaks or a sewer backup endorsement, are different, and we help separate them.</p>
<h2>Working with us</h2>
<p>We start with an inspection and a detailed, independent estimate. If the carrier's number is well short and your policy has an appraisal clause, we can serve as your appraiser and work toward an agreed figure or an umpire decision.</p>

<h2>Services available in Scranton</h2>
<p>Every Pennsylvania Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in Scranton and the rest of Lackawanna County, including:</p>
